{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-162564",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $x$-component of $F=⟨x+6y{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $x$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the front and back sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14128562/assets/a900f65b194e7992d3de6abfb078ff0c7cd1e2d163861d9ade810409d632693d.png)\n\n  \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the front surface. On this surface, we have $x=2$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨x+6y{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2+6y{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =2+6y{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2+6y{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2+6y{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2+6y{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2y+3{y}^{2}{z}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2+3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[2z+{z}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the back surface. On this surface, we have $x=0$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨x+6y{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6y{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-6y{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-6y{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}6y{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}6y{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{[{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[{z}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3+(-1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
